# QEMU Cortex-M33 carrier presenting the SF32LB57X chip identity.
#
# Why a board per series, rather than project-supplied compiler defines:
#
#   * Kconfig symbols, not defines, are what the SDK branches on. drv_spi_flash.c
#     guards HAL_FLASH_NOP_CMD with "#ifndef SOC_SF32LB55X" -- a -DSF32LB55X on
#     the command line does not satisfy it, so the wrong branch is compiled and
#     the failure surfaces as an unrelated-looking header error. Selecting the
#     chip here puts SOC_SF32LB57X in rtconfig.h where that code expects it.
#
#   * The include order is not something a project can get right on its own.
#     bf0_hal.h does #include "register.h" by name, so the chip's register map
#     has to precede the carrier's own drivers_qemu/register.h; get it wrong and
#     the error reads as a missing register map. The carrier fixes the order
#     centrally, in rtos/rtthread/bsp/sifli/drivers_qemu/SConscript.
#
#   * The chip headers and the HAL macros they need (SF32LB57X, SOC_BF0_HCPU,
#     USE_HAL_DRIVER) normally come from SConscripts under drivers/, which
#     SOC_QEMU skips wholesale. The carrier supplies the equivalents.
#
#   * One board symbol per carrier, with the chip identity separate from it.
#     The build gates on SOC_QEMU (drivers/, FlashDB, middleware/system), but
#     "which board is this build for" has to be answerable on its own: a
#     testcase names its platform by board name, and the .config is what says
#     which carrier produced it.
#
# Nothing here compiles a HAL implementation. The chip identity is declarations
# only -- which is what lets a CMock-generated mock be the sole definition of a
# HAL symbol on this carrier.
config BSP_USING_BOARD_QEMU_SF32LB57X
    bool
    select SOC_QEMU
    select SOC_SF32LB57X
    select BF0_HCPU
    default y

rsource "../Kconfig.board"
